    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Within the nucleus of the eukaryotic cell, DNA is packaged into chromatin, a compact yet dynamic structure consisting of DNA wrapped around histones to form nucleosomes.  Chromatin remodelers are the protein complexes responsible for the repositioning and restructuring of nucleosomes, an essential process for regulating access to the genome.  Previous research on INO80, a chromatin remodeler conserved from Saccharomyces cerevisiae to humans, has shown that one module of the complex (consisting of two protein subunits, Arp5 and Ies6) can exist as a separate subcomplex in the cell, capable of binding to chromatin independently.  This Arp5 subcomplex appears to be essential for regulation of several key metabolic genes in yeast.  This study examines how Arp5 and Ies6 bind to each other and to the full INO80 remodeling complex, as well as how the subcomplex is loaded onto chromatin by the full complex, the mechanism of which has yet to be determined.  Through immunoprecipitation experiments in yeast, it was determined that the C-terminus of Ies6 is critical for complex assembly.  Through chromatin fractionation experiments, we show that the deposition of the subcomplex is dependent upon the Nhp10 module of INO80, but independent of the catalytic activity of the Arp8 module.  Chromatin remodeling is an important means of epigenetic regulation, and the discovery of the Arp5 subcomplex presents a novel method of chromatin regulation by remodeling complexes.  Further research aims to reveal the presence of other subcomplexes and elucidate their distinct chromatin remodeling functions.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
